{
  "title": "Signal to Save",
  "text": "Rain hammers the windows as the clock ticks toward midnight. The cracked relay sputters your own weary voice, a warning looping on a loop, then pauses for breath. You flip the toggle to channel nine, the safe bypass you marked on the maintenance diagram, and key in the coordinates you copied: Grid C-7. The transmitter hums, then steadies, sending the coordinates into the night.

Static crackles, then a calm dispatch voice answers. “Copy, Grid C-7 received. Launching rescue now.” A moment later, the faint thrum of another boat’s engine cuts through the rain‑soaked air, distant but growing. You watch the station clock edge past 23:55; the relay continues its eerie replay, but the future you heard does not materialize.

At 23:58, dispatch radios back: “Both technicians aboard Kestrel, safe. Return to port.” Relief settles in the cramped cabin. You flip the main shutdown switch, the lights dim, and the station falls silent just before the stroke of midnight."
}

{
  "title": "The Manifest in the Rain",
  "text": "You flip the thin, damp page of the maintenance boat manifest under the sputtering lamp. The ink has bled slightly where the rain has seeped through the cracked window, but the names are still legible. 

The header reads **Kestrel**, and beneath it two technicians are listed: **Marin Delgado** and **Eli Voss**. Their scheduled return time, 23:20, is crossed out in red—clearly missed. No other entries follow; the paper ends with a blank space where a signature would be. You note the names in the log, the same way you recorded the chart coordinates, but you do not call anyone in. The technicians remain absent, their presence confined to the manifest alone.

Outside, the rain hammers the breakwater, and the low notes from the damaged relay pulse on repeat, each tone a thin echo of your own exhausted voice. Your hand hovers over the shutdown switch, the minutes ticking down toward midnight."
}

{
  "title": "Tracing the Relay",
  "text": "You flick the lamp on the maintenance board, the paper diagram illuminated by the desk lamp's amber glow. Lines of ink trace the station's signal path: the main carrier frequency runs straight into the battered relay, then loops back into the transmitter. A small handwritten note in the margin catches your eye: “Use channel nine – bypass damaged relay.” The rain hammers the windows, a dull percussion that blurs the breakwater outside, while the receiver continues its low, mournful chords. You jot the instruction in the log beside the coordinate entry, the ink dark against the already‑filled page. The clock ticks toward midnight, each second a reminder of the twenty‑minute gap you logged earlier. You keep your hand steady on the shutdown switch, aware that you have identified a safe transmission route but you do not press the key. The station hums around you, the future voice still echoing, waiting for a decision you have not yet made."
}

{
  "title": "Echoes in the Log",
  "text": "You stare at the digital clock flashing 23:40, the rain drumming a steady rhythm against the cracked window. The fixed receiver crackles, replaying your own exhausted voice—older, strained—begging you not to shut the line. Three low notes linger, then a breath fills the silence.

You pull the station log from the metal tray, the paper still warm from the lamp. With a steady hand, you compare the timestamp stamped on the recording with the clock on the wall. The tape reads 00:00, exactly twenty minutes ahead of the present time. Your pen scratches a line across the page: "Recording timestamp 00:00, station clock 23:40 – discrepancy noted. No transmission sent yet."

The recorder continues its loop, the notes echoing in the dim, salty air. Your hand hovers over the shutdown switch, the decision looming as the rain darkens the breakwater beyond the glass."
}

{
  "title": "Midnight's Quiet",
  "text": "Rain hammers the glass as the clock ticks toward twelve. The cracked relay repeats your own exhausted voice, pleading not to turn the transmitter off, then a breath and three low notes linger in the air. You glance at the maintenance diagram, the bold ink of the bypass note—'Use channel nine'—still fresh, and at the log where you’ve noted the twenty‑minute discrepancy and the coordinates of the powerless Kestrel, Grid C‑7. The future warning sits in your notebook, a possible transmission that has not yet been sent. Your hand hovers over the shutdown switch, the metal cool under the dim lamp. With a decisive squeeze, you snap the circuit, the hum of the transmitter dying in a soft pop. The repeated voice fades, the notes dissolve into the rain‑filled night. You gather your notes, lock the logbook, and step out into the hallway, leaving the station to close in silence as the minute hand slides past midnight. The fate of the technicians remains unknown."
}

{
  "title": "Echoes From Tomorrow",
  "text": "You press the earphone against your ear and let the receiver run. The crackle settles into a clear, weary version of your own voice, recorded twenty minutes ahead of the clock. \"If you're hearing this,\" it says, \"the main frequency has failed. I tried to patch the relay, but the burst overloaded the transmitter. The signal cut out at 23:58, and the boat drifted beyond the breakwater with no power.\" The words are a warning, not a report of something already done. The timestamp on the playback reads 23:58, confirming the twenty‑minute gap you logged. The tone is urgent but calm, a future you might avert. The rain hammers the windows, the low notes repeat once, then a breath pauses before the message ends. You note the details in your log, the safe channel nine route still untouched, and the switch remains under your hand, waiting."}

{
  "title": "Diagram of the Dilemma",
  "text": "Rain hammers the pane as you flip the log open, noting the timestamp that sits twenty minutes ahead of the station clock. The hum of the receiver fills the cramped room, the three low notes echoing like a distant foghorn. You slide the maintenance diagram from its sleeve, its edges damp from the humidity. Tracing the lines with a fingertip, you see the main transmission frequency looping back into the damaged relay, a feedback loop that could amplify any signal sent through it. A scribbled note in the margin catches your eye: “Use channel nine – bypass relay.” The instruction is clear, the safe route mapped. You memorize the channel, the coordinates of the drifting boat still unconfirmed, and the fact that no transmission has left the station yet. The shutdown switch glints under the flickering lamp, but you keep your hand poised, the decision to act hanging in the stale, salty air as the clock ticks toward midnight."
}

